---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Related from "../../components/Related.astro";

const title = "Why we don't publish liquidation totals";
const description =
  "Exchange liquidation feeds are throttled, so published liquidation totals are systematically understated — by up to roughly 100x during the cascades that make them newsworthy. This explains the mechanism and why this site shows no total.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>Why we don't publish liquidation totals</h1>
  </div>
  <p class="callout">
    Almost every crypto data site publishes a "total liquidated" figure. We do not, because the
    feeds those numbers are built from are throttled, and the resulting understatement is largest
    at exactly the moments the number is most quoted.
  </p>

  <h2>The mechanism</h2>
  {/* THE QUOTE AND ITS PARAPHRASE DISAGREED, AND BINANCE HAS SAID BOTH. This quoted "only the latest one
      liquidation order within 1000ms will be pushed" and two sentences later paraphrased it as
      "everything except the largest one in each second is discarded" — time and size, two rules with
      opposite biases (audit, 16 September 2026, https://coinliqui.com/methodology/liquidations).
      Checked the same day: developers.binance.com answers scripted requests with an HTTP 202 challenge,
      so the wording was traced through the description Binance ships in its own generated client,
      binance/binance-connector-python, file clients/derivatives_trading_usds_futures/src/
      binance_sdk_derivatives_trading_usds_futures/websocket_streams/streams/market_api.py:
      "latest" at 8b4e2c0834 (2026-03-16); "largest" at f37bdb7bb7 (2026-04-20), 30bb512ff7 (2026-06-09)
      and 9f368a902b (2026-06-26); "latest" again at 0362ef1909 (2026-07-14) through 7a5500f573
      (2026-08-18, master on 16 September). binance-connector-js master also reads "latest". The USDⓈ-M
      change log, as captured on 6 August 2026 in a public copy, has one entry, 10 April 2026: changed
      "latest" to "largest"; it records no change back. So the page no longer names a survivor rule as
      settled: it quotes both wordings with dates and draws only the conclusion that holds under either
      — one order per symbol per second survives, so a sum is a lower bound.
      RE-CHECKED 16 SEPTEMBER 2026, AND LINKED. The audit also found the quote undated and the body
      linking nowhere a reader could check it. gh api over binance-connector-python confirmed every
      commit above (latest / largest ×3 / latest ×4, master latest) and binance-connector-js master
      (clients/derivatives-trading-usds-futures/src/websocket-streams/modules/market-api.ts: "latest",
      six times). The change log at the URL linked below, read through a browser-grade fetch the same
      day (newest heading 2026-09-15), carries the 2026-04-10 entry and none reversing it. The two links
      are the stream description Binance's own client cites and that change log; the sentence asserts
      only what a script could re-read, which is why it names the client libraries, not the web page. */}
  <p class="prose">
    Binance's liquidation stream <code>!forceOrder@arr</code> does not emit every liquidation. Its
    {" "}<a href="https://developers.binance.com/en/docs/catalog/core-trading-derivatives-trading-usd-s-m-futures/api/ws-streams/market#all-market-liquidation-order-streams" rel="noopener" target="_blank">description</a>
    {" "}says the stream pushes a <strong>snapshot</strong>: for each symbol, one liquidation
    order within 1000ms, and <em>"if no liquidation happens in the interval of 1000ms, no stream will
    be pushed"</em>. Which order survives has not been stated consistently. The description read
    <em>"only the latest one liquidation order within 1000ms will be pushed"</em> until 10 April 2026,
    when Binance's <a href="https://developers.binance.com/en/docs/products/derivatives-trading-usds-futures/change-log" rel="noopener" target="_blank">change log</a> records "latest" being changed to "largest";
    the description Binance ships in its own client libraries was back to "latest" by July 2026 and
    still read that way when checked on 16 September 2026.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    One message per symbol per second is ample when the market is quiet and one liquidation
    arrives every few minutes. During a cascade, when dozens or hundreds of positions are closed
    per second on a single symbol, all but one of them in each second are discarded — the most
    recent or the largest, depending on which wording holds. Neither rule rescues the sum: keeping
    the largest shrinks the shortfall, it does not remove it. A sum over that stream is therefore
    not a total. It is a sample, and the sampling rate falls as the event gets larger.
  </p>

  <h2>The measured scale</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Vetle Lunde of K33 Research put it plainly in August 2024: liquidation data from exchanges is
    <em>"bogus and a vast underrepresentation of actual liquidation volumes in the market"</em>.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    In October 2025 Hyperliquid's founder described the same throttling and estimated the
    understatement at up to roughly <strong>100x</strong> during peaks. The crash of
    <strong>10 October 2025</strong> is the clearest illustration: headline coverage reported
    roughly <strong>$19 billion</strong> liquidated market-wide, while Hyperliquid alone — which
    publishes complete records because it settles on-chain — accounted for
    <strong>$10.31 billion</strong> of it. One venue cannot be 54% of a market it is not 54% of.
    The market-wide figure was too low, not Hyperliquid's too high.
  </p>

  <h2>Why we don't simply use a better source</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    The obvious answer is to take liquidations from a venue that publishes them completely.
    Hyperliquid settles on-chain, so in principle every liquidation is a public event. In practice
    its public API exposes no market-wide liquidation feed: liquidation events are delivered only
    inside a per-address subscription, which requires knowing in advance which address was
    liquidated. Reconstructing the market-wide series means indexing every block, which is a
    permanent piece of infrastructure rather than a page.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    So the choice is between publishing a number we know is wrong, publishing one we cannot
    produce, or publishing neither and saying why. We publish neither and say why.
  </p>

  <h2>What we do publish</h2>
  {/* THE GAP RUNS BOTH WAYS, SO THE SENTENCE NO LONGER PICKS ONE. This said the common formula "ignores
      maintenance margin entirely and is wrong by the maintenance fraction, which grows as position size
      crosses tier boundaries", then linked "Every contract page prints both results and the gap between
      them". Audit of 16 September 2026: on 21 of 50 contract pages the printed gap ran the other way —
      https://coinliqui.com/funding/aero, 3× cap, 16.667% maintenance: 20.00% below entry against 10.00%
      for the formula, "more room than the formula shows, because the tier caps the leverage at 3×";
      thirteen 5× contracts 11.11% against 10.00% — and all 21 have a single tier, so nothing crosses a
      boundary. Those pages price a 10× long; margin.ts clamps 10× to the tier cap and roomVerdict() says
      which way the gap runs. Under a cap Le below the requested L the room is 1/(2Le−1) against the
      formula's 1/L, so a cap below (L+1)/2 gives more room and a cap above it less: the direction depends
      on the contract, the leverage and the size, and this paragraph now says only that. "Steps up at each
      boundary" was checked against src/data/margin-tables.json the same day: max leverage falls at every
      boundary in all ten tables (six have more than one tier). The link says "which of the two leaves
      more room", not "which way it runs": roomVerdict() has a fourth answer, "same", and the contract
      page then prints "the same room the formula shows" — a gap with no direction, but still an answer. */}
  <p class="prose">
    Liquidation <strong>price</strong> — where a given position would be closed — is fully
    computable, because the maintenance-margin tiers are published. That is a different quantity
    from liquidation <strong>events</strong>, and it is genuinely useful: the
    {" "}<code>entry × (1 − 1/leverage)</code> formula in circulation on most calculators ignores
    the tiers entirely, and it can miss in either direction. It ignores maintenance margin, which
    closes a position while some of its margin is still left, and that alone brings liquidation
    nearer to entry than the formula says. It ignores the tier's leverage cap, and a position capped
    below the leverage asked for carries more margin, which moves liquidation further away. Which of
    the two wins depends on the contract, the leverage and the position size; on a contract with more
    than one tier, the cap steps down and the maintenance requirement steps up each time the size
    crosses a boundary.
    {" "}<a href="/funding/btc">Every contract page prints both results, the gap between them and which of the two leaves more room →</a>
  </p>

  <h2>What would change this</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    A verified extraction path that yields market-wide liquidation events within the cost
    constraints of this site, or funding sufficient to run a block indexer. Until one of those
    exists, this page is the answer rather than a placeholder for a section.
  </p>

